===============
Grammar Parsing
===============

Grammars can be parsed from strings:

    >>> from nltk import CFG
    >>> grammar = CFG.fromstring("""
    ... S -> NP VP
    ... PP -> P NP
    ... NP -> Det N | NP PP
    ... VP -> V NP | VP PP
    ... Det -> 'a' | 'the'
    ... N -> 'dog' | 'cat'
    ... V -> 'chased' | 'sat'
    ... P -> 'on' | 'in'
    ... """)
    >>> grammar
    <Grammar with 14 productions>
    >>> grammar.start()
    S
    >>> grammar.productions()
    [S -> NP VP, PP -> P NP, NP -> Det N, NP -> NP PP, VP -> V NP, VP -> VP PP,
    Det -> 'a', Det -> 'the', N -> 'dog', N -> 'cat', V -> 'chased', V -> 'sat',
    P -> 'on', P -> 'in']

Probabilistic CFGs:

    >>> from nltk import PCFG
    >>> toy_pcfg1 = PCFG.fromstring("""
    ... S -> NP VP [1.0]
    ... NP -> Det N [0.5] | NP PP [0.25] | 'John' [0.1] | 'I' [0.15]
    ... Det -> 'the' [0.8] | 'my' [0.2]
    ... N -> 'man' [0.5] | 'telescope' [0.5]
    ... VP -> VP PP [0.1] | V NP [0.7] | V [0.2]
    ... V -> 'ate' [0.35] | 'saw' [0.65]
    ... PP -> P NP [1.0]
    ... P -> 'with' [0.61] | 'under' [0.39]
    ... """)

Chomsky Normal Form grammar (Test for bug 474)

    >>> g = CFG.fromstring("VP^<TOP> -> VBP NP^<VP-TOP>")
    >>> g.productions()[0].lhs()
    VP^<TOP>

Grammars can contain both empty strings and empty productions:

    >>> from nltk.grammar import CFG
    >>> from nltk.parse.generate import generate
    >>> grammar = CFG.fromstring("""
    ... S -> A B
    ... A -> 'a'
    ... # An empty string:
    ... B -> 'b' | ''
    ... """)
    >>> list(generate(grammar))
    [['a', 'b'], ['a', '']]
    >>> grammar = CFG.fromstring("""
    ... S -> A B
    ... A -> 'a'
    ... # An empty production:
    ... B -> 'b' |
    ... """)
    >>> list(generate(grammar))
    [['a', 'b'], ['a']]

Grammars with mixed rules can be converted into Chomsky Normal Form:

    >>> from nltk import CFG
    >>> grammar = CFG.fromstring("""
    ... S -> NP VP
    ... PP -> P NP
    ... NP -> NP PP P
    ... NP -> 'the' Nom | 'a' Nom
    ... VP -> V NP | VP PP
    ... Det -> 'a' | 'the'
    ... Nom -> 'dog' | 'cat'
    ... V -> 'chased' | 'sat'
    ... P -> 'on' | 'in'
    ... """)
    >>> grammar
    <Grammar with 15 productions>
    >>> grammar.chomsky_normal_form()
    <Grammar with 18 productions>

Probabilistic Chomsky normal form
--------------------------------

``PCFG.chomsky_normal_form()`` preserves each finite string's probability,
summed over all parses, without modifying the input. Already-CNF grammars are
returned unchanged. Otherwise, unreachable and zero-probability rules are removed.

The construction retains the four steps from `Ruoxi Ning's original explanation
<https://github.com/nltk/nltk/pull/3382#issuecomment-2742800451>`_:

* START: if the start symbol occurs on a RHS, add a fresh ``S0 -> S [1.0]``.
* TERM: replace a terminal in a longer rule with a fresh lexical auxiliary,
  e.g. ``S -> 'a' B [p]`` becomes ``S -> T B [p]``, ``T -> 'a' [1.0]``.
* MUL (BIN): ``S -> A B C [p]`` becomes ``S -> A X [p]``, ``X -> B C [1.0]``.
  Use fresh auxiliaries for each alternative, even when prefixes are shared.
* UNIT: multiply weights along unary paths, add paths to the same ordered RHS,
  then remove unreachable rules.

Here is the original UNIT example:

    >>> grammar = PCFG.fromstring("""
    ... A -> B [0.3] | B C [0.7]
    ... B -> C [0.8] | B C [0.2]
    ... C -> 'c' [0.5] | D [0.5]
    ... D -> 'd' [1.0]
    ... """)
    >>> cnf = grammar.chomsky_normal_form()
    >>> for rule in sorted(cnf.productions(), key=str):
    ...     print(rule)
    A -> 'c' [0.12]
    A -> 'd' [0.12]
    A -> B C [0.76]
    B -> 'c' [0.4]
    B -> 'd' [0.4]
    B -> B C [0.2]
    C -> 'c' [0.5]
    C -> 'd' [0.5]

Each lexical exit from ``A`` has weight ``0.3 * 0.8 * 0.5 = 0.12``;
``A -> B C`` has weight ``0.7 + 0.3 * 0.2 = 0.76``. ``D`` becomes unreachable.
The unary paths are acyclic, although the binary rule ``B -> B C`` is recursive.

Unary cycles use the hitting-probability construction in Appendix B, Lemma B.13
of Etessami, Stewart and Yannakakis, `Polynomial Time Algorithms for Multi-Type
Branching Processes and Stochastic Context-Free Grammars
<https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.2374v2>`_. Let ``U[A,B]`` sum the probabilities of
``A -> B``, and ``B[A,gamma]`` sum direct probabilities of each non-unary RHS.
Summing all unary paths gives ``X = B + U B + U**2 B + ...``, or ``X = U X + B``.
For one RHS, this is the prototype's linear system ``(I - U) x_gamma = b_gamma``.

The implementation uses state elimination: sum self-loops geometrically, then
substitute their remaining transitions into other equations. A global inverse
can be singular with closed unary classes; the paper solves on states that can
reach the chosen exit. Missing exit mass is retained through a fresh dead symbol
``D -> D D [1.0]``, as in the lemma. For ``S -> A [0.5] | 'x' [0.5]; A -> A [1]``,
the probability of ``'x'`` stays 0.5. Conditioning on termination would change it.

Structural steps multiply weights by ones; UNIT sums paths to the same ordered
RHS. Thus they preserve finite-string probabilities in exact arithmetic, but
can change tree structure and Viterbi scores. No NumPy dependency is required.
``flexible=True`` keeps unary rules. Empty productions remain unsupported;
the paper's epsilon elimination and Newton-method approximation are not included.

Conversion of non-CNF inputs requires finite, nonnegative weights summing to one
per LHS within ``1e-12``, stricter than the constructor's tolerance. Self-loop
elimination sums the remaining mass to avoid cancellation in ``1 - loop``.
Exit totals are checked with the same tolerance; roundoff just above one is
clipped. Positive rules have no cutoff, but floating-point underflow and rounding
still limit accuracy.

The grammar reader accepts the default ``@$@`` padding. Include the start symbol
when serializing, since production order need not identify it:

    >>> text = '% start ' + str(cnf.start()) + '\n'
    >>> text += '\n'.join(map(str, cnf.productions()))
    >>> restored = PCFG.fromstring(text)
    >>> restored.start() == cnf.start() and restored.productions() == cnf.productions()
    True

``nltk/test/unit/test_pcfg_cnf.py`` checks full acyclic distributions by derivation
enumeration, geometric unary cycles, closed classes and Catalan parse counts.
An independent Markov walk checks 81 two-state and 12 four-state matrices with
lexical and ordered binary exits. Unary mass per row is at most one half, so
the omitted mass after 80 steps is at most ``2**-80`` in exact arithmetic;
floating-point tolerances are separate. These tests are motivated by Lemma B.13,
not supplied by the paper. Other regressions cover small probabilities, roundoff,
symbol collisions, parser compatibility and serialization.
